WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.394
Welcome to Module 3 of the
Microsoft Automation learning path:

2
00:00:04.394 --> 00:00:06.650
Intune through Microsoft Graph.

3
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:12.215
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Devices
and Endpoints book moves the fleet from

4
00:00:12.215 --> 00:00:17.162
Group Policy to Intune, and then has to
prove, continuously, what each device is.

5
00:00:17.162 --> 00:00:22.424
In this module you'll map the workbook's
device evidence questions to the Graph API

6
00:00:22.424 --> 00:00:25.984
that answers each one, its
least-privileged permission,

7
00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:29.388
and whether it's in version
one point oh or only in beta.

8
00:00:29.388 --> 00:00:31.796
Then you'll run five read-only checks,

9
00:00:31.796 --> 00:00:35.249
in Python and PowerShell,
against a fictional fleet.

10
00:00:35.249 --> 00:00:40.188
They complement the validation kit's Intune
checks by reading Intune's own records.

11
00:00:40.188 --> 00:00:43.161
Like Module 2's checks,
they're illustrative:

12
00:00:43.161 --> 00:00:47.480
tested against fixtures, not
proven against live tenants.

13
00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.380
Why does this matter?

14
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.755
Your Conditional Access policies
already require a compliant device.

15
00:00:56.755 --> 00:01:00.804
That's only as good as Intune's
compliance records, and by default,

16
00:01:00.804 --> 00:01:04.930
a device with no compliance policy
assigned counts as compliant.

17
00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:08.142
Microsoft recommends changing
that to not compliant,

18
00:01:08.142 --> 00:01:10.568
and targeting every device with a policy.

19
00:01:10.568 --> 00:01:15.462
The workbook's device evidence guidance
asks for evidence a script can collect:

20
00:01:15.462 --> 00:01:19.467
which devices fail which policy,
which devices no policy reaches,

21
00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:23.991
which stopped checking in, which aren't
encrypted or are below the OS floor,

22
00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:27.329
and which Autopilot registrations
won't get a profile.

23
00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:30.601
The validation kit already
has six Intune checks,

24
00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:34.830
reading Entra device objects,
group tags and assignment design.

25
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.680
This module doesn't repeat them.

26
00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.460
Its checks read Intune's own records.

27
00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.998
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:45.998 --> 00:01:48.660
which takes about eighty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.328
You'll name the Graph API,
least-privileged permission and

30
00:01:52.328 --> 00:01:56.072
stage for each of the workbook's
device evidence questions.

31
00:01:56.072 --> 00:01:59.872
You'll explain which three things
the program relies on are beta-only.

32
00:01:59.872 --> 00:02:03.574
You'll read managed devices and
per-policy compliance status,

33
00:02:03.574 --> 00:02:06.260
including why the checks
join on device name.

34
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:10.535
You'll keep remote actions and export
jobs out of a read-only collector.

35
00:02:10.535 --> 00:02:15.860
And you'll say what differs in GCC
High and DoD, and run the five checks.

36
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.985
You need Module 1, and Module 2
for the Conditional Access side.

37
00:02:19.985 --> 00:02:24.980
Lab 2 needs a test tenant with an
Intune license and a few test devices.

38
00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.708
Here are six of the
workbook's device evidence

39
00:02:31.708 --> 00:02:34.842
questions with the Intune
API that answers each.

40
00:02:34.842 --> 00:02:39.726
Enrolled devices, with their OS, compliance
state, encryption and last sync,

41
00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:44.217
come from managed devices, with the
managed devices read permission.

42
00:02:44.217 --> 00:02:48.458
Compliance policies, their assignments
and their per-device statuses

43
00:02:48.458 --> 00:02:52.267
need the configuration read
permission, in version one point oh.

44
00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:55.592
The Settings Catalog and
endpoint security policies,

45
00:02:55.592 --> 00:02:59.804
and assignment filters, use the same
permission, but they're beta only.

46
00:02:59.804 --> 00:03:04.718
Autopilot registrations are in version one
point oh with the service configuration

47
00:03:04.718 --> 00:03:07.070
read permission, but whether each one has

48
00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.630
been given a deployment
profile is beta only.

49
00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:15.640
And every Intune API in Graph needs an
active Intune license for the tenant.

50
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:24.028
Microsoft supports the Intune beta
APIs, but says they're subject to more

51
00:03:24.028 --> 00:03:28.665
frequent change, and recommends version
one point oh where it can be used.

52
00:03:28.665 --> 00:03:31.803
Three things the program
relies on are beta-only today.

53
00:03:31.803 --> 00:03:36.143
The Settings Catalog: the workbook's
Intune policy library is mostly

54
00:03:36.143 --> 00:03:40.940
Settings Catalog profiles, so exporting
the library as code depends on beta.

55
00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.525
Assignment filters: the
filter list is beta,

56
00:03:43.525 --> 00:03:47.691
and version one point oh can't tell
you which filter an assignment uses,

57
00:03:47.691 --> 00:03:52.215
which is why the validation kit's filter
check needs its include-beta switch.

58
00:03:52.215 --> 00:03:56.406
And Autopilot profiles: version
one point oh lists registrations,

59
00:03:56.406 --> 00:04:00.977
but the profile assignment status
and the profiles themselves are beta.

60
00:04:00.977 --> 00:04:04.522
As design guidance, re-run
a beta-dependent check after

61
00:04:04.522 --> 00:04:08.430
each beta change before you
use its result as evidence.

62
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.868
A managed device is Intune's
record of an enrolled device.

63
00:04:15.868 --> 00:04:18.338
The checks read its compliance state,

64
00:04:18.338 --> 00:04:23.519
one of seven values from compliant to in
grace period; its last successful sync;

65
00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:27.504
its OS version and encryption
status; and its Entra device ID,

66
00:04:27.504 --> 00:04:30.055
the join key to the Entra device object.

67
00:04:30.055 --> 00:04:34.830
The same record also returns a person's
name, email address and phone number.

68
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.864
The checks name only what they need
in select and leave those out,

69
00:04:38.864 --> 00:04:42.518
so a check's output never carries
a person's name or address.

70
00:04:42.518 --> 00:04:44.055
That's design guidance.

71
00:04:44.055 --> 00:04:48.161
And watch for defaults that look like
data: some properties, such as the

72
00:04:48.161 --> 00:04:52.692
MAC address and physical memory, return
default values when you list devices.

73
00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:57.170
Their real values come only from
reading one device and selecting them.

74
00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.982
A compliance policy's
assignments list its targets.

75
00:05:03.982 --> 00:05:07.694
In version one point oh a group
target carries a group ID,

76
00:05:07.694 --> 00:05:10.357
and beta documents an exclusion target.

77
00:05:10.357 --> 00:05:15.275
A policy with no assignments, or with
only exclusions, applies to no device,

78
00:05:15.275 --> 00:05:19.708
and remember, by default an unassigned
device counts as compliant.

79
00:05:19.708 --> 00:05:23.339
Each policy's device statuses
give one row per device,

80
00:05:23.339 --> 00:05:26.970
with a status such as
non-compliant, error or conflict.

81
00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.933
But the row carries a device
name, and its ID is described

82
00:05:30.933 --> 00:05:34.982
only as the key of the entity,
not as the managed device's ID.

83
00:05:34.982 --> 00:05:38.372
So the checks join status
rows to devices on the name,

84
00:05:38.372 --> 00:05:41.132
and flag names held by
more than one device.

85
00:05:41.132 --> 00:05:43.845
That's an inference,
and an open review item.

86
00:05:43.845 --> 00:05:46.719
Two tenant-wide settings
sit above every policy;

87
00:05:46.719 --> 00:05:51.590
their read permission wasn't confirmed
on Learn, so the checks don't read them.

88
00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:58.990
Two things look useful and don't
belong in a read-only collector.

89
00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:02.878
Remote actions: retire, wipe
and even sync device all need

90
00:06:02.878 --> 00:06:07.428
the privileged operations
permission, delegated or application.

91
00:06:07.428 --> 00:06:09.239
That's an operator's permission.

92
00:06:09.239 --> 00:06:13.195
A collector that holds it could wipe
the fleet, so as design guidance,

93
00:06:13.195 --> 00:06:16.515
keep it in a separate pipeline
with its own approval step.

94
00:06:16.515 --> 00:06:20.706
And report export jobs: creating
one needs a read-write permission,

95
00:06:20.706 --> 00:06:24.517
with no read-only option listed,
and the file it produces sits

96
00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:28.327
on blob storage behind a signed
address, not on the Graph host.

97
00:06:28.327 --> 00:06:31.728
The example client won't send
its token anywhere else.

98
00:06:31.728 --> 00:06:35.278
So the checks page through
the list APIs instead.

99
00:06:35.278 --> 00:06:38.668
A hundred policies cost about
two hundred and one requests,

100
00:06:38.668 --> 00:06:43.400
well under Intune's limit of a thousand
per twenty seconds per app per tenant.

101
00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:48.200
Five checks.

102
00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.441
Noncompliant devices lists
devices that are noncompliant,

103
00:06:52.441 --> 00:06:56.512
in grace, in error or in conflict,
with the failing policies.

104
00:06:56.512 --> 00:07:01.630
Compliance coverage lists policies
with no assignment or only exclusions,

105
00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.824
and devices no policy reports on.

106
00:07:03.824 --> 00:07:08.463
Stale check-in lists devices with no
Intune sync inside the fourteen-day

107
00:07:08.463 --> 00:07:12.250
validity period or the ninety-day
cleanup age, or none at all.

108
00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:17.138
Device posture lists Windows and Mac
devices not reporting encryption,

109
00:07:17.138 --> 00:07:19.175
and devices below the OS floor.

110
00:07:19.175 --> 00:07:21.513
And Autopilot profiles, on beta,

111
00:07:21.513 --> 00:07:26.100
lists registrations whose profile
isn't assigned and in sync.

112
00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:31.167
Every control they evidence is in the
NIST Moderate baseline: configuration

113
00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:35.838
settings, the baseline, the inventory,
encryption, and flaw remediation.

114
00:07:35.838 --> 00:07:39.312
The mapping is the program's
reading; the assessor decides.

115
00:07:39.312 --> 00:07:41.690
The permissions are a reader's set.

116
00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.165
Try it.

117
00:07:46.165 --> 00:07:49.541
Lab 1 runs each check
against a fictional fleet

118
00:07:49.541 --> 00:07:53.090
of nine devices and six
Autopilot registrations.

119
00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.552
Here's step two, compliance coverage.

120
00:07:55.552 --> 00:08:00.261
Two policies reach no device: the
kiosk policy has only an exclusion,

121
00:08:00.261 --> 00:08:03.289
and an old Mac pilot has
no assignment at all.

122
00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:07.196
Two devices, a kiosk and a Mac,
have no policy reporting on them,

123
00:08:07.196 --> 00:08:11.102
so with the tenant's default
setting they count as compliant.

124
00:08:11.102 --> 00:08:14.788
And W S zero zero zero two
is flagged as ambiguous:

125
00:08:14.788 --> 00:08:19.764
two devices share that name, so a
status row could belong to either.

126
00:08:19.764 --> 00:08:25.940
Step six asks the client to read the
Autopilot collection as a GCC High run would.

127
00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:30.742
It returns not available, with the
reason, and makes no request, because the

128
00:08:30.742 --> 00:08:35.702
call list follows Intune's government
service description, not the API page.

129
00:08:35.702 --> 00:08:40.190
The lab repeats two checks in
PowerShell, with the same results.

130
00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:49.440
Lab 2 runs the checks read-only against
your own test tenant, worldwide or GCC,

131
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:54.678
never production, with an Intune
license and a few enrolled test devices.

132
00:08:54.678 --> 00:08:58.789
Register one app in the tenant's
own cloud, with a certificate.

133
00:08:58.789 --> 00:09:01.642
Grant only the read permissions
for the checks you run,

134
00:09:01.642 --> 00:09:04.240
and never the privileged
operations permission.

135
00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.288
Run each task in Python, or connect
in PowerShell and run it there;

136
00:09:08.288 --> 00:09:11.878
a missing permission stops
the run before any Graph call.

137
00:09:11.878 --> 00:09:15.836
Compare the results with the
Intune admin center's compliance,

138
00:09:15.836 --> 00:09:20.175
device and Autopilot views, and record
the module's open review items,

139
00:09:20.175 --> 00:09:23.753
such as whether a status row's
ID matches a managed device.

140
00:09:23.753 --> 00:09:28.348
Then back it out: remove the permissions,
delete the app registration and its

141
00:09:28.348 --> 00:09:30.901
certificate, and delete the saved results,

142
00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:33.527
which hold device names
and serial numbers.

143
00:09:33.527 --> 00:09:36.030
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

144
00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:41.092
Where are agencies?

145
00:09:41.092 --> 00:09:46.773
The agency the workbook's device plan
describes manages Windows with Group Policy,

146
00:09:46.773 --> 00:09:51.630
and often Configuration Manager, and
is moving to Intune in a GCC tenant.

147
00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.555
GCC has no separate Intune instance.

148
00:09:54.555 --> 00:09:58.822
In Microsoft's words, GCC is
the same instance as Intune in

149
00:09:58.822 --> 00:10:03.180
the commercial space, and it
uses the worldwide Graph host.

150
00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:08.696
GCC High and DoD share a separate government
instance, with their own Graph hosts,

151
00:10:08.696 --> 00:10:14.130
and moving between instances means
unenrolling and re-enrolling every device.

152
00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:18.057
Microsoft's service description
lists what isn't available there,

153
00:10:18.057 --> 00:10:22.483
with no plans to add it: Windows Autopilot,
Remediations, feature update and

154
00:10:22.483 --> 00:10:27.267
compatibility reports, the ServiceNow
connector and Subscription Activation.

155
00:10:27.267 --> 00:10:31.715
Standard device management, compliance,
configuration and app policies,

156
00:10:31.715 --> 00:10:37.170
is available, with Log Analytics export
and Defender security settings management.

157
00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:42.395
One discrepancy to know.

158
00:10:42.395 --> 00:10:48.058
The Graph reference pages for Autopilot
device identities list the API in the GCC

159
00:10:48.058 --> 00:10:51.889
High and DoD clouds, while
Intune's service description

160
00:10:51.889 --> 00:10:55.220
lists Windows Autopilot
as not available there.

161
00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:58.790
The service description decides
whether the feature can be used;

162
00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:02.095
an endpoint that answers doesn't
make the feature supported.

163
00:11:02.095 --> 00:11:06.755
That's an inference, and the kit's call
list follows the stricter statement,

164
00:11:06.755 --> 00:11:10.345
so the Autopilot check is for
GCC and commercial tenants.

165
00:11:10.345 --> 00:11:14.434
The interim paths: run compliance
coverage until it lists nothing,

166
00:11:14.434 --> 00:11:17.483
then change the tenant's
default to not compliant.

167
00:11:17.483 --> 00:11:20.585
Devices still on Group
Policy aren't in Intune yet,

168
00:11:20.585 --> 00:11:24.294
so track them with the validation
kit's platform inventory.

169
00:11:24.294 --> 00:11:28.140
In GCC High or DoD, Chapter
04 of the Devices and

170
00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:32.895
Endpoints book compares
Autopilot device preparation.

171
00:11:32.895 --> 00:11:36.300
And label the Settings
Catalog export as beta.

172
00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:41.437
The federal requirement.

173
00:11:41.437 --> 00:11:46.522
Every control the checks evidence is in
the NIST special publication eight hundred

174
00:11:46.522 --> 00:11:50.734
fifty-three Moderate baseline:
configuration baseline and settings,

175
00:11:50.734 --> 00:11:54.583
the component inventory, protection
of information at rest and

176
00:11:54.583 --> 00:11:58.650
encryption on mobile devices, and
flaw remediation for the OS floor.

177
00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:04.795
Through Entra ID, compliance is the
device-level signal that M-22-09 asks agencies

178
00:12:04.795 --> 00:12:07.159
to consider when authorizing users,

179
00:12:07.159 --> 00:12:10.625
and that ScubaGear's
managed-device policy relies on.

180
00:12:10.625 --> 00:12:13.788
ScubaGear has no Intune
baseline of its own.

181
00:12:13.788 --> 00:12:18.977
Appendix A of the Zero Trust and
Assessment book has the M-22-09 mapping,

182
00:12:18.977 --> 00:12:23.188
and Chapter 10 of Devices and
Endpoints says what an assessor reads.

183
00:12:23.188 --> 00:12:26.796
The evidence an ISSO keeps:
each check's dated output,

184
00:12:26.796 --> 00:12:31.661
with its evidences list and the beta
label; the coverage result and the

185
00:12:31.661 --> 00:12:37.230
changed default; and encryption findings
confirmed in Intune's own report.

186
00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.955
Check yourself with two questions.

187
00:12:42.955 --> 00:12:47.455
First: a compliance policy's only
assignment is an exclusion group.

188
00:12:47.455 --> 00:12:48.755
Which check reports it?

189
00:12:48.755 --> 00:12:51.380
The answer is b, compliance coverage.

190
00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:54.443
A policy with only exclusions
reaches no device,

191
00:12:54.443 --> 00:12:58.042
and by default an unassigned
device counts as compliant.

192
00:12:58.042 --> 00:13:03.068
Second: what does the Autopilot
check report in a GCC High tenant?

193
00:13:03.068 --> 00:13:06.418
The answer is c: not evaluated,
without calling Graph,

194
00:13:06.418 --> 00:13:12.030
because Intune's service description lists
Windows Autopilot as not available there.

195
00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:17.300
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

196
00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:21.875
To sum up.

197
00:13:21.875 --> 00:13:26.145
You mapped the workbook's device
evidence questions to Intune APIs,

198
00:13:26.145 --> 00:13:29.500
their least-privileged
permissions and their stage.

199
00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.075
You learned which three things the
program relies on are beta-only.

200
00:13:33.075 --> 00:13:35.845
You read managed devices
and compliance status,

201
00:13:35.845 --> 00:13:38.200
and saw why the checks
join on device name.

202
00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:41.850
You kept remote actions and
export jobs out of the collector.

203
00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:47.613
And you ran the five checks, and saw why
Autopilot is a GCC and commercial check.

204
00:13:47.613 --> 00:13:49.548
To put the checks on a schedule,

205
00:13:49.548 --> 00:13:52.876
go to the Intune track
implementation guide's Gate 6.

206
00:13:52.876 --> 00:13:57.945
Next is Module 4, Azure Arc through Azure
Resource Manager and Resource Graph,

207
00:13:57.945 --> 00:14:00.638
which leaves Graph for the server estate.

208
00:14:00.638 --> 00:14:04.820
With it, the Microsoft Automation
learning path has four modules,

209
00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.763
about five hours and
fifty-five minutes in all.

210
00:14:07.763 --> 00:14:11.980
Modules on Teams, and on monitoring
and evidence, are planned.
